Stretch — remediation plan for the most significant gap

Work package: lawful basis, public-source transparency, and sensitive-data boundary

Artifact that closes the gap: A signed processing-purpose register and legitimate-interests assessment for bounded public-source research, accompanied by Articles 13/14 privacy notices, an Article 14 exception decision where relevant, and a written rule that prohibits sensitive-person research, criminal-data processing, unrelated profiling, cross-client reuse, and model training unless separately approved.

Owner: The controller's product owner prepares the processing map; the DPO/privacy lead owns the LIA and notices; engineering implements input warnings, person-level filters, source restrictions, and deletion/objection controls; external EU privacy counsel reviews unresolved Article 9, 10, or 14 questions.

Realistic timeline: Ten business days for the first complete package if vendors and data flows are already known. Pause the clock when material vendor, purpose, or transfer facts remain unavailable. Do not enable real-person production processing before written approval.

Closure evidence: Approved purpose register and LIA; dated DPO/legal sign-off; published and in-product notices; recorded Article 14 delivery/exception logic; tests showing prohibited inputs and uses are blocked or routed for review; a source/recipient register; a successful objection/deletion exercise; and change-control evidence that any new purpose triggers reassessment.

